    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.72) and **Josh Naylor** (71.15) lead our lineup, both benefiting from favorable matchups against pitchers with xERAs above 4.40. **Tyler Soderstrom** specifically thrives in a hitter-friendly park with a +10% park factor, and his placement in the three-hole maximizes his RBI potential against Mitch Keller. Meanwhile, **James Wood** (71.11) remains a mandatory start; despite facing a capable veteran in Michael Wacha, his elite 0.407 xwOBA and the +10% boost from leadoff placement make him our most potent engine for Runs and counting stats. **Brice Turang** (66.63) also warrants special mention as a high-upside play; the combination of a favorable matchup against Slade Cecconi and a significant boost from the opponent's slow delivery\u2014which plays directly into our strategy of chasing stolen bases\u2014makes him a key contributor.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Kyle Karros**, whose score of 44.15 narrowly missed the cut. While he projects as a serviceable bat, he was penalized by the optimizer for his projected spot at the bottom of the order and the difficulty of facing Edward Cabrera, whose 3.98 xERA limits the ceiling for a standard utility start. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Corbin Carroll** (51.08) and **Gabriel Moreno** (43.72), as both are staring down tough matchups against Reid Detmers. The 3.19 xERA for the opposition significantly suppresses their expected efficiency, and with lineup statuses still pending, we are monitoring the situation to ensure these players aren't forced into unfavorable defensive environments or dropped further down the order. \n\nBeyond the starters, we are keeping a close watch on **Dalton Rushing** (40.56), who sits right on the floor of our efficiency threshold. His matchup against Drew Rasmussen is a primary concern, as the 3.36 xERA suggests a difficult outing for a player currently relegated to the bottom of the order. We are also managing the status of **Mickey Moniak**, who remains unavailable due to his IL designation, while keeping an eye on **Jose Caballero** (52.17); while he offers potential, his -13% platoon penalty today serves as a reminder of the volatility we face when chasing speed in a crowded outfield rotation.",
